WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.560
There's a handful of subtle references to
films, real-world events, and even the silent

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.200
art of mime lurking in Joker's grim and gritty
Gotham City.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.460
If you're still reeling from the violence
and drama of Arthur Fleck's tragic journey,

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.300
keep watching to learn about the little things
you might've missed.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.280
If you know one piece of trivia about the
Joker, it's probably that he was originally

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.950
inspired not by the playing card from which
he took his name, but by Gwynplaine, the main

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.620
character of the 1928 silent film The Man
Who Laughs.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.780
In the movie, Gwynplaine, played by German
actor Conrad Veidt, is a child in the 17th

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.320
century whose father is killed by rival noblemen.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.100
Before they put his father in the Iron Maiden,
though, they disfigure his face into a permanent

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.180
smile, leaving him unable to ever truly match
his face to his emotions.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.780
Also, Gwynplaine's father was betrayed by,
wait for it, his jester, whose "jests were

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.940
cruel and his smiles were false."

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.560
Sound like anyone you know?

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.960
Fittingly enough, this cinematic inspiration
made its way back to the movies in Joker.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.540
The parallels to Arthur Fleck's medical condition,
which causes him to compulsively laugh whenever

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.660
he's under stress, are obvious.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.840
It's more than just the subtext, though - one
Easter egg is a visual nod to a specific shot

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.890
from The Man Who Laughs that recurs several
times in Joker.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.090
Throughout the film, Fleck forces a "smile"
onto his face by hooking his fingers into

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.840
his mouth and pulling upwards.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.720
This is exactly the same thing that the movie's
doctor does to demonstrate Gwynplaine's condition.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.420
Perhaps more importantly is the fact that
Fleck does this same thing to a young Bruce

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.909
Wayne when he's trying to get access to Thomas
Wayne - the lines immediately after that shot

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:36.639
in The Man Who Laughs explain that they did
it so that Gwynplaine would "laugh forever

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.160
at his fool of a father."

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.420
Given the dynamic in play between Fleck and
the Waynes, there's actually way more to it

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.400
than just the supremely creepy act of putting
your hands in a child's mouth.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.800
The Man Who Laughs isn't the only silent movie
to have its influence felt on Joker.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.620
In most versions of Gotham City, it's difficult
to imagine a well-publicized showing of a

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:02.350
famous Charlie Chaplin comedy that seems to
be attended solely by billionaires as anything

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.200
other than a terrible idea.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.660
Joker, however, takes place before Gotham
is overrun with grandstanding, theatrical

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:13.050
criminals who would strike at such at gathering,
so it probably seemed like a good idea at

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:13.720
the time.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.640
Still, it raises the question of why Modern
Times was chosen for a sequence in the film,

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.570
and was featured so prominently that we stop
to watch Arthur Fleck as he watches one of

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.160
its most famous gags.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.160
There's an obvious level, of course; the scene
in question involves Chaplin's character roller-skating

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.330
blindfolded, nearly falling over a ledge into
a precipitous fall, a pretty on-the-nose reinforcement

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.060
of Fleck as a man teetering on the edge of
sanity.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.660
If you're familiar with the rest of Modern
Times, though, you'll realize that some of

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.900
its themes are Easter eggs mirroring the ones
we're seeing play out for Gotham City.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.320
This is, after all, a film where Chaplin,
as the Tramp, is dealing with a desperate

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.250
economic situation, finding himself literally
chewed up and spat out by the machinery of

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.280
heavy industry.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.820
Even more telling is the scene where he's
arrested and sent to jail before being pardoned,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.020
only to argue that he'd rather stay in jail
than go back out and face the alternative.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.320
That's a sentiment that's echoed in Joker
by Brian Tyree Henry in his role as a file

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.540
clerk at Arkham State Hospital, who tells
Arthur that some people are better off locked

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.460
up there than out on the increasingly dangerous
streets of Gotham City.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.380
Saying that director Todd Phillips was inspired
by Martin Scorsese in Joker is sort of like

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.480
saying that life on Earth is "inspired by"
the sun.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.090
There's a very significant amount of Taxi
Driver in the DNA of Joker's broader plot

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:33.451
points, from Arthur getting the handgun from
his coworker, Thomas Wayne's political ambitions,

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.140
and the grimy feel of Gotham City.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.820
It's not the only Scorsese picture in play,
though.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:44.200
Perhaps unsurprisingly, 1983's The King of
Comedy had a huge influence on Joker, to the

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.060
point where it's worth your time to watch
just to catch everything that's going on.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.800
See if you can spot the connections here:
Robert De Niro stars as Rupert Pupkin, an

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.820
aspiring stand-up comedian obsessed with talk
show host Jerry Langford.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.280
Pupkin often has vivid fantasies, which are
never clearly delineated for the audience,

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.480
of being on Langford's show and even being
friends with Langford himself.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.360
"I really appreciate you meeting me for lunch.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.440
I know how busy you are, how tired you are…"

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.540
"What are friends for, Jerry?"

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:19.499
Eventually, his obsession leads him to kidnap
Langford, holding him for ransom until he's

00:04:19.499 --> 00:04:23.870
allowed to be a guest on the show, where he
tells a few jokes about his crime, confessing

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.420
to the audience in the guise of a stand-up
routine.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:31.260
If you've seen Joker, you should be experiencing
a powerful feeling of déjà vu right now,

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.650
but the exclamation point on the Easter egg
comes in De Niro himself being cast as Murray

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.660
Franklin, the talk show host with whom Fleck
is obsessed.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.800
Naturally, he winds up being caught up in
Fleck's madness, even accidentally giving

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:43.960
him the name "Joker."

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.680
The parallels are strong, even if Joker turns
out to be significantly more homicidal than

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.780
Pupkin was.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.380
The motif of Arthur Fleck dancing recurs throughout
Joker, from his halting, Oedipal waltz with

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.160
his mother all the way to the final moments
of the film.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.560
But there's one moment where it seems different.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.660
After his first trio of kills, when he's collecting
himself in a dimly lit bathroom, Fleck's feet

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:10.500
sweep across the floor, leading his whole
body into a long routine.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.240
The thing is, it doesn't look like the dancing
we see elsewhere in the film.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.680
It's more fluid, and seems more theatrically
performative.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.060
So what's the deal?

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.440
You can debate why Arthur chooses this moment
for a graceful dance sequence, but in the

00:05:23.449 --> 00:05:27.289
real world, the filmmakers could be using
the scene to evoke the fluid movements of

00:05:27.289 --> 00:05:29.680
legendary French mime Marcel Marceau.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.439
If that sounds like a long shot, that's because
it is, but consider this potential Easter

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:39.219
egg: one of Marceau's most famous performances
was The Maskmaker, in which he pantomimed

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.620
putting on a series of masks, changing his
expression to match.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:47.340
The climax of the piece comes when Marceau
finds that a "mask" depicting pure, wide-eyed

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.600
joy has become stuck on his face.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.200
He struggles to remove it, trying to peel
off what seems like his own skin as his body

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:58.949
contorts with pain and depression, all while
his face remains locked in the painted smile

00:05:58.949 --> 00:05:59.840
of a clown.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.560
If that doesn't sound like what's going on
in Joker, then we don't know what does.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.060
This might be surprising considering that
it's a movie about the Joker - you know, the

00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:12.129
bad guy from the Batman comic books? - but
Joker doesn't really have a lot of specific

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:13.810
references to the comics.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.099
Other than the major characters, there aren't
really any superheroes or villains referenced

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:22.409
in the film, unless you count the offhand
mention of "super cats" being a deep cut reference

00:06:22.409 --> 00:06:23.240
to Streaky.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.979
There's no hint of Harley Quinn, no Ha-Hacienda
or Jokermobile, and sadly, Arthur Fleck never

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.500
even proclaims that it's time for the Joker's
Five-Way Revenge!

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.820
There's one major exception, though.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:39.199
While it's pretty clearly an homage to King
of Comedy, it's easy to argue that the entire

00:06:39.199 --> 00:06:43.939
climax of the film is an Easter egg referencing
one of the most influential Batman comics

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:46.380
of all time: The Dark Knight Returns.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.640
In the third issue of Frank Miller's '80s
classic, the Joker appears as a guest on a

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.520
talk show.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.700
The scene in Joker isn't a direct homage to
the comics, seeing as how the circumstances

00:06:55.700 --> 00:07:00.100
of the stories are so different, but there
is one clear reference you may have missed.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:05.080
It's one of the show's other guests, Dr. Sally,
whose few lines indicate that she's a sex

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:08.500
therapist, along the lines of the real-life
Dr. Ruth Westheimer.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:13.260
A similar figure also appears in The Dark
Knight Returns, as does the theatrical and

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.560
incredibly creepy kiss that Joker plants on
her as he enters the scene.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.220
Joker marks the fifth time that a live-action
Mr. J has been featured on the big screen.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:29.860
The first four times, 1966, 1989, The Dark
Knight, and Suicide Squad if you're keeping

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.680
score at home, all featured very different
designs, but that all drew from the same source.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:39.180
You'd never confuse Cesar Romero's Joker with
Heath Ledger's, but they've both got green

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.620
hair and purple suits, and even Jared Leto's
purple snakeskin coat keeps the color scheme

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:44.440
going.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.360
Joker, on the other hand, breaks with tradition.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.110
Rather than the traditional purple, Arthur
Fleck's emergence as the Joker comes with

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:52.500
a slightly different look.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.960
The green hair is there, and the traditional
three-piece suit, but rather than purple and

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.640
green, it's more of a burgundy.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.080
It's a small difference, and the most likely
explanation is that Todd Phillips, Joaquin

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:07.460
Phoenix, and costume designer Mark Bridges
wanted their take on the character to be visually

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:12.119
distinct from the others, which was probably
a good idea given a few strong similarities

00:08:12.119 --> 00:08:13.439
to Heath Ledger's look.

00:08:13.439 --> 00:08:17.509
But there is one place where you can find
this color scheme on a past version of the

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:19.860
Joker, and it probably isn't where you expect.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:25.219
Back in 1990, the Kenner toy company was riding
the wave of the '89 Batman movie when they

00:08:25.219 --> 00:08:26.960
released "Sky Escape Joker."

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.020
It's not quite the same costume - the vest
and shirt colors are switched - but the color

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:35.380
of the jacket is shockingly similar to what
wound up in theaters 29 years later.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:39.850
Does this mean that there was a draft of Joker
in which Phoenix flew around on Sky Escape

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:41.480
Joker's backpack helicopter?

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.820
Probably not, but hey, you never know.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.200
Release the Sky Escape cut, Warner Bros.!

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.720
Joker pulls a big portion of its aesthetic
from New York City in the mid-'80s, and not

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.420
just because it's evoking the grimy, crime-ridden
streets of Taxi Driver and The King of Comedy.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:59.280
There are real-life incidents forming the
background for the events playing out on the

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.000
screen, including the massive spike in crime
that accompanied the crack epidemic, and a

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.970
"garbage strike" that's based on similar sanitation
worker strikes in New York, like one that

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.480
lasted 17 days in 1981.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.200
The one incident most heavily referenced in
the plot of Joker is the 1984 shooting of

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.740
four men in a subway car by Bernie Goetz.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:22.100
Goetz claimed that he was defending himself
from a robbery, and was initially dubbed the

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:25.180
"Subway Vigilante" by media before he turned
himself in.

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:29.580
He was also referred to as the "Death Wish
Vigilante," a reference to the popular revenge

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:31.440
movies starring Charles Bronson.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:35.900
Many New Yorkers, as well as people following
the story from elsewhere, were supportive

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:40.700
of Goetz's actions, viewing them as a justified
response to the seemingly unchecked rise of

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:41.800
violent crime.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.080
"A vigilante showing up every now and then
would have good, uh, a healthy reminder for

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.660
some of the lawbreakers out there."

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:49.890
"You're a lawyer?"

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:50.760
"I'm a lawyer."

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.000
Eventually, though, public opinion turned
against him, largely because of the view that

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:59.710
Goetz had been racially motivated - all four
men shot by Goetz were black, Goetz himself

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:03.640
was not - which was supported by racist statements
Goetz had made in the past.

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:07.800
Some of his statements also came off as needlessly
violent, and even creepy.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.480
"My problem is I ran out of bullets.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:13.320
And I was gonna gouge out one of the guy's
eyes out with my keys afterwards."

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:17.880
While there were plenty of people willing
to indulge in Death Wish-style revenge fantasies,

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.120
those are the kind of statements that make
them think you might not be all that stable

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:22.020
to begin with.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:26.780
It's no accident, then, that Arthur's transformation
into Joker begins with shooting his assailants

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:31.240
on a subway train, and that he only stops
shooting because he runs out of bullets, or

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:35.680
that his actions are sensationalized by the
media and approved of by others in Gotham

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:36.680
City.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.100
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.780
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:48.000
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

